      "id": 26,
      "title": "DM Draft: The First Fall",
      "type": "Generic",
      "categoryId": 4,
      "summary": "In First Age 1384 a star left its place and fell on the border of three peoples' lands. The churches recovered a dark husk from the crater, the count of the age was closed, and the Second Age began. The questions did not close with it.",
      "body": "In the last year of the First Age, a star fell. It is the only event in the counted years that everyone alive at the time seems to have seen, and the only one that ever ended an age. The First Age had run 1,384 years from the opening of [[The First Ledger and the Start of Counting|the First Ledger]]. It closed with a light in the sky, a hole in the highlands, and an argument that has not finished yet.\n\n## The night of the fall\n\nThe keepers of [[Time]] recorded a clear night in the year 1,384, and a star missing from its place the night after. Between those two entries, the star crossed the whole sky, bright enough to read as a second, brief dawn, and struck in high country where the lands of three peoples meet. The ground-quake was felt in dwarven holds and in camps on the steppe alike. Where it landed, the stone of the crater floor had run like water and set again as glass.\n\nPeople from the nearest villages reached the crater first, and put out the grass fires, and waited there, sensibly, for somebody important to arrive and tell them what they had seen.\n\n## The search\n\nSomebody important arrived in force. Because the site lay between peoples, and because what had fallen might be a power, the claim fell under the charter of [[The Founding of the Conclave|the Conclave]], and the search was a joint work: church parties from every faith with an interest, delegates of the three peoples, and dwarven clerks to keep the record of it.\n\nThe search lasted a season. The crater was sifted, the glass was carted, the rim was camped on by three peoples who did not fight, which old hands at the site will tell you was the charter's first real test, and its first real victory.\n\n## What was found\n\nAt the center, under the glass, lay the husk.\n\nThe accounts describe a dark, rounded stone, small enough for two people to carry and heavier than its size promised, cold to the touch in full sun. Priests of every church attended it in turn, over the days that followed. It gave no answer, no warmth, and no sign of power to any of them.\n\nThe report of the Conclave, which is quoted in every later account, concludes:\n\n> \"The husk, whole and entire, was taken up from the crater and delivered into the keeping of the Conclave, there to remain under seal.\"\n\nThe dwarven inventory of the crater, kept with the report, reads in part:\n\nCrater glass, fused stone, and ash: nine cartloads, weighed.\nThe husk: one piece, forty-one stone.\nA third line is ruled beneath, and left blank.\n\nThe husk has been moved three times in the years since, from one Conclave vault to another, each move sealed and witnessed, and each move has produced a fresh story that it was stolen in the moving. The seals, as far as the record shows, have never been broken.\n\n## What it was\n\nThe cautious reading, held by most scholars, is that one of the smallest [[Shards]], thrown out at the fracture to become a star, had come back to the ground. See [[The Uncounted Time]]. A star falling is a shard landing, and a spent one might well land silent.\n\nThe priests of [[Light and Dark]] argue that it was a piece of the sun, which pours out the power of Fire and Light, and that their church therefore holds an interest in it. It has never given light or heat, which their priests answer by saying that a spent piece would not.\n\nThe strongest feelings belong to the human scholars, who argued in print, and argue still, that the fallen thing was the Missing Shard, come back at last to its people. Against this the record is plain: the husk answered no one, it worked nothing, and the silence of the human [[Patrons|Patron]] continued after the fall exactly as it had run before it. The human case is set out in [[The Borrowed Faiths of Humans]].\n\n## The turning of the age\n\nAges do not usually end because of a stone. This one did, for a practical reason: the fall belonged to no people's reign, calendar, or story, and every court and church in the world needed the same before and after to argue about it in. When the dwarven auditors completed the final audit of the crater accounts, they closed the count of the First Age at 1,384 years, and the next new year opened as Year One of the Second Age. The First Age is only called first because a second one began. At the time, people called it simply the count.\n\n## The crater today\n\nThe crater keeps its glass, and the glass keeps leaving, in pilgrims' pockets and in the packs of dealers who sell it in market towns as fall glass. A small Conclave station stands at the rim, keeping copies of the search records and receiving the curious. The husk itself rests under seal in a Conclave vault, and it has now kept its silence for twenty-six years.",
      "visibility": "draft",
      "tags": [
        "history",
        "first fall",
        "second age",
        "star",
        "conclave",
        "husk"
      ],
      "pinned": false,
